WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.340
How does a cue only five to ten percent stronger on one side turn a whole axon?

2
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:15.180
In 1979, Gundersen and Barrett placed a pipette releasing nerve growth factor near

3
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.600
chick sensory axons.

4
00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:21.203
Within about twenty minutes, the growth cones turned toward the pipette.

5
00:00:23.617 --> 00:00:25.277
One growth cone wanders.

6
00:00:25.897 --> 00:00:29.437
So the evidence is the spread of turning angles across many cones.

7
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:35.270
In a control dish with a uniform bath of cue, the spread centres on zero.

8
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.130
Now apply the rule to each side.

9
00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:42.630
In one common picture, an attractant lets the near side gain more.

10
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.080
It may add actin faster there, ease the backward flow, or engage more clutch.

11
00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.240
A repellent does the reverse.

12
00:00:53.383 --> 00:00:56.063
The gaining side puts out more filopodia and sheets.

13
00:00:56.923 --> 00:00:58.203
The fan shifts that way.

14
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:03.713
Microtubules from the centre enter the gaining side more often, and stay longer.

15
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:10.323
Behind them the fan narrows, and that stretch becomes axon shaft, pointing the new

16
00:01:10.323 --> 00:01:11.323
way.

17
00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:15.473
Signalling inside the cell can enlarge the small difference.

18
00:01:16.393 --> 00:01:18.793
Repeated biased growth then adds up to a turn.

19
00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:25.673
So stabilize the microtubules on one side, with no cue at all.

20
00:01:26.453 --> 00:01:28.013
Which way does the growth cone turn?

21
00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:38.273
Toward the drug. Buck and Zheng found this in frog spinal neurons.

22
00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:42.597
The near side protruded first, and the turn needed actin.

23
00:01:43.597 --> 00:01:46.437
Microtubules can start a turn by working through actin.

24
00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:51.990
Break microtubules down on one side, with a high enough dose, and the cone turns

25
00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:52.990
away.

26
00:02:01.083 --> 00:02:04.963
Last question. Does the tip need its cell body to decide?

27
00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:12.457
In 2001, Campbell and Holt cut frog retinal axons, leaving growth cones with no cell

28
00:02:12.457 --> 00:02:13.457
body.

29
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.560
The isolated tips still turned in gradients of guidance cues.

30
00:02:20.083 --> 00:02:25.123
their assay, turning needed protein made from messenger RNA already at the tip.

31
00:02:26.083 --> 00:02:28.063
Not every cue needs new protein.

32
00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:32.803
Chick sensory growth cones turned toward nerve growth factor without it.

33
00:02:35.117 --> 00:02:37.957
So the tip can decide on its own, at least in a dish.

34
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:44.510
Carry the rule. A turn sticks where microtubules move in and build axon, and they

35
00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:45.510
can start it too.

36
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:52.580
The cues that make the sides differ, netrins, slits, semaphorins and ephrins, come

37
00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:52.880
next.
